    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(8))  # F0..F7\n    customers = list(range(6))  # C0..C5\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(33, 66), (63, 79), (69, 87), (87, 38), (88, 75), (51, 62), (52, 88), (65, 25)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(2, 98), (54, 79), (9, 25), (79, 66), (28, 98), (51, 59)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [185.0, 121.0, 189.0, 102.0, 164.0, 72.0, 138.0, 96.0]\n    customer_demands = [11.0, 13.0, 46.0, 18.0, 50.0, 24.0]\n    fixed_costs = [296.0, 204.0, 229.0, 349.0, 211.0, 377.0, 206.0, 101.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.5  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Warehouse location and distribution plan \u2014 decision brief\n\nTeam,\n\nWe need to determine which of our candidate warehouses to open and how to route product to customers while keeping total costs down. This decision spans eight potential warehouse sites and six customer locations. Our objective is straightforward: meet every customer\u2019s demand without exceeding warehouse capacities, and do so at the lowest combined cost of opening and shipping.\n\nKey inputs for this decision:\n- The candidate warehouse map coordinates are provided in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (33, 66)\n  - F1: (63, 79)\n  - F2: (69, 87)\n  - F3: (87, 38)\n  - F4: (88, 75)\n  - F5: (51, 62)\n  - F6: (52, 88)\n  - F7: (65, 25).\n- Customer delivery points and their map coordinates are listed in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (2, 98)\n  - C1: (54, 79)\n  - C2: (9, 25)\n  - C3: (79, 66)\n  - C4: (28, 98)\n  - C5: (51, 59).\n- Warehouse capacity limits are summarized in [185.0, 121.0, 189.0, 102.0, 164.0, 72.0, 138.0, 96.0].\n- Customer demand requirements are detailed in [11.0, 13.0, 46.0, 18.0, 50.0, 24.0].\n- The fixed cost to open each warehouse is given in [296.0, 204.0, 229.0, 349.0, 211.0, 377.0, 206.0, 101.0].\n- The per\u2011unit transport rate for shipping is 0.5.\n\nTo avoid ambiguity, distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates. In practice, we\u2019ll measure the direct point\u2011to\u2011point distance between each warehouse and each customer using their coordinates, and apply the transport rate to that distance and the shipped volume to determine shipping cost.\n\nBusiness rules to observe:\n- A warehouse must be opened before it can ship any product.\n- Every customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied; no shortfalls.\n- Total shipments from any open warehouse cannot exceed its capacity.\n- Shipping costs scale with the Euclidean distance and the number of units shipped, based on the transport rate.\n\nThe decision we\u2019re making is twofold: which warehouses to open, and how much to ship from each open warehouse to each customer. The goal is to minimize total cost\u2014the sum of facility opening costs and shipping costs\u2014while meeting all customer demand and respecting capacity limits.\n\nThe outcome of this work will be a recommended set of warehouses to activate, a shipment plan that allocates customer volumes to those sites, and a clear view of the resulting total cost. Once aligned, we can implement the plan and monitor performance against expectations.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Warehouse location and distribution plan \u2014 decision brief\n\nTeam,\n\nWe need to determine which of our candidate warehouses to open and how to route product to customers while keeping total costs down. This decision spans eight potential warehouse sites and six customer locations. Our objective is straightforward: meet every customer\u2019s demand without exceeding warehouse capacities, and do so at the lowest combined cost of opening and shipping.\n\nKey inputs for this decision:\n- The candidate warehouse map coordinates are provided in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}.\n- Customer delivery points and their map coordinates are listed in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}.\n- Warehouse capacity limits are summarized in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}.\n- Customer demand requirements are detailed in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}.\n- The fixed cost to open each warehouse is given in {FIXED_COSTS}.\n- The per\u2011unit transport rate for shipping is {TRANSPORT_RATE}.\n\nTo avoid ambiguity, distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates. In practice, we\u2019ll measure the direct point\u2011to\u2011point distance between each warehouse and each customer using their coordinates, and apply the transport rate to that distance and the shipped volume to determine shipping cost.\n\nBusiness rules to observe:\n- A warehouse must be opened before it can ship any product.\n- Every customer\u2019s demand must be fully satisfied; no shortfalls.\n- Total shipments from any open warehouse cannot exceed its capacity.\n- Shipping costs scale with the Euclidean distance and the number of units shipped, based on the transport rate.\n\nThe decision we\u2019re making is twofold: which warehouses to open, and how much to ship from each open warehouse to each customer. The goal is to minimize total cost\u2014the sum of facility opening costs and shipping costs\u2014while meeting all customer demand and respecting capacity limits.\n\nThe outcome of this work will be a recommended set of warehouses to activate, a shipment plan that allocates customer volumes to those sites, and a clear view of the resulting total cost. Once aligned, we can implement the plan and monitor performance against expectations."
